 "stimulus_html": "Find the payment for the following student loans using the standard repayment plan:",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 519076,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Loan is $7,800, interest is 4.99%",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "$82.70"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>The standard plan is 12 payments a year for 10 years.</p>\n<p>To compute the payment to pay down a loan with beginning principal, <i>P</i>, the payment (<i>pmt</i>) is <span data-math=\"pmt = \\frac{{P \\times \\frac{r}{n} \\times {{\\left( {1 + \\frac{r}{n}} \\right)}^{n \\times t}}}}{{{{\\left( {1 + \\frac{r}{n}} \\right)}^{n \\times t}} - 1}}\"></span>, where <i>r</i> is the annual interest rate (in decimal form), <i>n</i> is the number of compounding periods per year, and <i>t</i> is the number of years. Note, payment to lenders is always rounded up to the next penny.</p>\n<p>If the number of periods per year is 1, then the formula is simplified to <span data-math=\"pmt = \\frac{{P \\times r \\times {{\\left( {1 + r} \\right)}^t}}}{{{{\\left( {1 + r} \\right)}^t} - 1}}\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Once again, it will help you get the right answer from your calculator to find <span data-math=\"n \\times t\"></span> first.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"n \\times t = \\left( {12} \\right)\\left( {10} \\right) = 120\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"pmt = \\frac{{\\left( {7,800 \\times \\frac{{0.0499}}{{12}} \\times {{\\left( {1 + \\frac{{0.0499}}{{12}}} \\right)}^{120}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{{\\left( {1 + \\frac{{0.0499}}{{12}}} \\right)}^{120}} - 1} \\right)}} = \\$82.70\"></span></p>\n<p>Additional parentheses were added to help you enter the expression properly in your calculator.</p>\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
